WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.744
Zero Trust considers the device involved in an access request because a

00:00:04.744 --> 00:00:09.387
valid identity using a poorly protected endpoint may still create significant risk.

00:00:09.387 --> 00:00:12.483
Identity confidence and device confidence answer different questions.

00:00:12.483 --> 00:00:16.740
Device posture can include whether the endpoint is known, managed, supported,

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:19.836
properly configured, encrypted, and reporting current security information.

00:00:19.836 --> 00:00:24.093
The exact signals depend on organizational policy, technology, and resource sensitivity.

00:00:24.093 --> 00:00:27.188
A registered device is not automatically safe forever.

00:00:27.188 --> 00:00:31.832
Configuration can drift, security tools can stop reporting, software can become outdated,

00:00:31.832 --> 00:00:33.380
and ownership can change.

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:37.250
Current posture is more useful than a one-time enrollment record.

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:40.346
Organizations may distinguish among corporate-managed, personally owned, partner-managed,

00:00:40.346 --> 00:00:41.893
shared, and specialized devices.

00:00:41.893 --> 00:00:48.085
Each category can have different levels of assurance and may receive different access to sensitive resources.

00:00:48.085 --> 00:00:50.020
Access decisions should be proportionate.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:54.277
A low-risk informational service may tolerate a broader range of devices,

00:00:54.277 --> 00:01:00.081
while administrative functions or sensitive data may require a managed endpoint with stronger posture evidence.

00:01:00.081 --> 00:01:02.790
When a device does not meet policy,

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:07.434
the response does not always need to be a simple permanent denial.

00:01:07.434 --> 00:01:11.691
Depending on risk and business need, the organization may limit access,

00:01:11.691 --> 00:01:15.947
require another verification step, direct remediation, or provide an approved alternative.

00:01:15.947 --> 00:01:19.043
Endpoint visibility supports monitoring after access is granted.

00:01:19.043 --> 00:01:21.365
Changes in device health, unusual activity,

00:01:21.365 --> 00:01:26.009
or loss of management coverage can trigger reevaluation and help security teams

00:01:26.009 --> 00:01:28.331
understand whether continued access remains appropriate.

00:01:28.331 --> 00:01:32.200
Device evaluation must also respect privacy, legal, and workforce requirements.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:36.457
Governance should define which signals are collected, why they are needed,

00:01:36.457 --> 00:01:39.940
how they are protected, and who may use them.
